  Blockers — must be done before helm install

  1. Register the EKS OIDC provider in IAM — the cluster has an OIDC issuer
  (0C6FDC371A7908E4289E0EDFDEFBC572) but it is not registered as an IAM identity provider yet.
  IRSA (pod-level IAM roles) won't work without this.
  2. Create the AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy IAM policy — not present in the account yet.
  3. Create IRSA IAM role + Kubernetes ServiceAccount for the LB controller — ties the IAM policy
   to the controller pod via the OIDC provider.
  4. Install aws-load-balancer-controller — not running in the cluster; no eks Helm repo added
  yet. Without it, ingressClassName: alb Ingress objects will be created but never acted on (no
  ALB will appear).
  5. Update subnet annotation to use subnet ID — the annotation currently says
  chatapp-subnet-public1-us-east-1a (name). The LB controller is more reliable with the actual
  ID. From the VPC lookup: chatapp-subnet-public1-us-east-1a = subnet-0e132fb27f601c9f1. I'll
  update the chart.
  6. Generate and set api.secretKey in values-dev.yaml (or values file) — currently the default
  placeholder "change-me-in-production…" is used. Fine to do now before deploying.

Actual deployment:
# Get the TLS thumbprint from the OIDC endpoint
   THUMBPRINT=$(echo | openssl s_client \
     -servername oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com \
     -connect oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com:443 2>/dev/null \
     | openssl x509 -fingerprint -noout -sha1 \
     | sed 's/SHA1 Fingerprint=//' \
     | tr -d ':' \
     | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')
   echo "Thumbprint: $THUMBPRINT"

   aws iam create-open-id-connect-provider \
     --url https://oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/0C6FDC371A7908E4289E0EDFDEFBC572 \
     --client-id-list sts.amazonaws.com \
     --thumbprint-list "$THUMBPRINT" 2>&1
   Register EKS OIDC provider in IAM


   curl -sL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/mai
   n/docs/install/iam_policy.json \
     -o /tmp/alb-iam-policy.json && echo "Downloaded $(wc -c < /tmp/alb-iam-policy.json)
   bytes"

   aws iam create-policy \
     --policy-name AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \
     --policy-document file:///tmp/alb-iam-policy.json \
     --query "Policy.{Arn:Arn,Name:PolicyName}" \
     --output table 2>&1
   Download and create ALB controller IAM policy


   Bash command
  
   cat > /tmp/alb-trust-policy.json << 'EOF'
   {
     "Version": "2012-10-17",
     "Statement": [
       {
         "Effect": "Allow",
         "Principal": {
           "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::866317130608:oidc-provider/oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.
   com/id/FABE8D4516C57785E724775331AA0733"
         },
         "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
         "Condition": {
           "StringEquals": {
             "oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/FABE8D4516C57785E724775331AA0733:aud":
   "sts.amazonaws.com",
             "oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/FABE8D4516C57785E724775331AA0733:sub":
   "system:serviceaccount:kube-system:aws-load-balancer-controller"
           }
         }
       }
     ]
   }
   EOF

   aws iam create-role \
     --role-name AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole \
     --assume-role-policy-document file:///tmp/alb-trust-policy.json \
     --query "Role.{Arn:Arn,Name:RoleName}" \
     --output table 2>&1
   Create IRSA IAM role with OIDC trust policy



   aws iam attach-role-policy \
     --role-name AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole \
     --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::866317130608:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy 2>&1 &&
   echo "Policy attached"

   kubectl create serviceaccount aws-load-balancer-controller \
     -n kube-system \
     --dry-run=client -o yaml \
     | kubectl annotate -f - \
       eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn=arn:aws:iam::866317130608:role/AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControll
   erRole \
       --local -o yaml \
     | kubectl apply -f - 2>&1
